---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const repo = 'https://github.com/natanatan/Emergent-Epistemic-Ledger';
const blob = `${repo}/blob/main`;
const crates = [
  ['eel-core', 'Typed ids, canonical serialization, every protocol object and event'],
  ['eel-crypto', 'Ed25519 identities, event signing and verification, local keystore'],
  ['eel-ontology', 'The state projector: every validation rule and the projected ontology'],
  ['eel-ledger', 'Append-only event log, Merkle trees, blocks, historical and branch reconstruction'],
  ['eel-validation', 'Genesis validators, domain authority, admission and reputation, canonicalization rules'],
  ['eel-simulation', 'Deterministic relational-graph simulator and replay verification'],
  ['eel-xchange', 'Research-request structures and outcome-independent credit (no currency)'],
  ['eel-storage', 'SQLite persistence, append-only at the database level'],
  ['eel-api', 'REST API and the eel-node binary'],
  ['eel-cli', 'The eel command-line client'],
];
---
<Base title="Code" description="The EEL prototype: a Rust workspace implementing the ledger, ontology projector, validation and deterministic simulation.">
  <p class="kicker">Open source · Apache-2.0</p>
  <h1>Code</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A locally runnable Rust prototype. Claims, evidence, objections, replications and simulations are
    signed events in an append-only ledger, and every node projects the same ontology from them.
  </p>
  <div class="actions">
    <a class="button primary" href={repo}>Open the repository</a>
    <a class="button" href={`${blob}/ARCHITECTURE.md`}>Read the architecture</a>
  </div>

  <h2>Run it</h2>
  <p>You need a recent stable Rust toolchain.</p>
  <pre><code>git clone {repo}.git
cd Emergent-Epistemic-Ledger
cargo test                                   # the full test suite, including a three-node test
cargo run --bin eel -- identity create       # a local identity on the development network
cargo run --bin eel -- claim create examples/claim.yaml
cargo run --bin eel -- ontology graph
cargo run --bin eel-node -- start            # REST API on http://127.0.0.1:7878</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>examples/walkthrough.sh</code> runs every success criterion of the specification through the CLI:
    two researchers, a claim, an objection, a revision, competing branches, domain-limited validation,
    a research request, a simulation replayed by two validators, independent replications, and a
    canonicalization that needs two validators.
  </p>

  <h2>What is where</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Crate</th><th scope="col">Contents</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {crates.map(([name, what]) => (
          <tr><td><a href={`${repo}/tree/main/crates/${name}`}><code>{name}</code></a></td><td>{what}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Documentation</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={`${blob}/docs/protocol.md`}>Protocol</a>: events, signatures and how the ontology is projected</li>
    <li><a href={`${blob}/docs/decisions.md`}>Decisions</a>: every place the specification left open, and the choice made</li>
    <li><a href={`${blob}/docs/threat-model.md`}>Threat model</a></li>
    <li><a href={`${blob}/ROADMAP.md`}>Roadmap</a></li>
    <li><a href={`${blob}/CONTRIBUTING.md`}>Contributing</a></li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Prototype only.</strong> The development network's validator keys are public. Do not use EEL for anything that needs security.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
